(BVI) and rotor–wake interaction with nearby infrastructure. The need to understand and resolve brownout, in which the rotor wake entrains sand or other particulates and convects them into the pilot’s visual field, has become critical for safety in desert environments. Sling load dynamics and shipboard operations require further understanding of the interaction of the wake with its environment. Strong interest in rotor wakes comes from the wind turbine community, driven by the need for knowledge on convection and dissipation of individual wakes in wind farms. The rotor wake can be deconstructed into three major “fields”: near, mid, and far, as illustrated in Fig. 1. The near field includes the region where the wake initially leaves the rotor blade and completes formation of the tip vortices. The accurate solution of this region is important to the prediction of blade airloads, BVIs, rotor vibration, and aeroacoustic signatures. The midfield encompasses the helicopter fuselage. Wake resolution is important to characterize rotor–fuselage interactions (RFIs), interior noise, and empennage buffeting. The far field includes the region where wake–environment interactions such as ground effect, brownout, shipboard operations, and formation flying are important. This paper summarizes the state of knowledge and advances with respect to rotor wakes from both experimental and computational perspectives, building on previous surveys of the field. A starting point is the review of helicopter aerodynamics done by Phillippe et al. (Ref. 1) in 1985, as part of an AGARD (Advisory Group for Aerospace Research and Development) volume on Aeromechanics. A more recent perspective comes from Conlisk (Ref. 2) published in 1997, the same year that the American Helicopter Society conducted a Technical Specialists’ Meeting on rotorcraft aeromechanics and acoustics. A history of computational developments for rotorcraft by Strawn (Ref. 3) in 2006 provides a recent perspective. In March 2009, the U.S. Army Research Office sponsored a workshop at the Georgia Institute of Technology (Ref. 4). This paper
