strated, but it was still far too slow and expensive to be used as industry tool. Advances in computing power and algorithms have now rendered Reynolds-averaged Navier–Stokes (RANS) calculations viable even for use some design stages of rotorcraft. The issues in blade computational fidelity have shifted to turbulence modeling for the wake and separated rotational flows. Meanwhile, new strides are being made in characterizing the wake and related flow field experimentally and with analytically based computational tools. Application of accurate nonintrusive measurement techniques in facilities, where vortex–wall interactions are minimized, have enabled clean measurements of vortex core structure in axial and in edgewise flight to large wake ages. With clear evidence from such experiments, analytical tools have progressed to open the way toward simulation of flight conditions where wake behavior at long ages is important. A recent U.S. Army Research Office (ARO) workshop, held in March 2009, posed eight questions about the status of wake modeling to researchers interested in rotorcraft and wind turbine wakes: 1) What is the structure of the TV at its origin, and what factors influence it? 2) How much of the blade bound vorticity actually ends up in the TV? 3) How fast does the TV diffuse/dissipate/decay? 4) What is the role of turbulence in these processes? 5) What are the physical phenomena responsible for observed “vortex jitter”? 6) What is the state of computational capability for the TV as a function of age in hover and forward flight? 7) How well are long-age phenomena, such as ground vortex rollup and tail rotor/main rotor interaction, captured? 8) What is the status of turbulence modeling for helicopter rotor wakes?
Experimental Advancements
The wake of a rotor is at once an extremely complex flow field, and one where the dominant phenomena are amenable to simple description. While by no means exhaustively including all of the research that has been performed, Table 1 classifies available experimental databases and Table 2 lists several of the issues that have been addressed during these experimental campaigns. The Gray deconstruction (Refs. 5, 6) of the hover wake vorticity structure into tip vortices and helical vortex sheets of the 1950s, and the Landgrebe extension (Ref. 7) into the complex interactions of forward flight developed in the early 1960s, still hold true to a very large extent. Several other details and implications have been proven since then. Many of the early results were obtained by
