mutual vortex interactions included by Landgrebe also occur in the hover case as the wake ages. The tip vortices roll around each other, in behavior that is similar in appearance at any given cross-section to the interaction of two corotating vortices in a two-dimensional shear layer. These are strongly helical vortices, however, and thus the stretching associated with this rollup must have strong influences. The rollup proceeds until the cores come close together and then lose definition in the merger. It is logical to expect turbulent processes to become significant at this stage, and that the strong axial flow slows down, with an accompanying rapid growth in core size. The merged vortices may then weaken much quicker than their components did before merger, and this delineates the start of the “far wake.” The discrete structures in the far wake may then break down, as occurs in shear layer growth. While these processes look very complex as the number of blades increases, they still obey deterministic descriptions until the vortex mergers occur. It is not known whether this deterministic, periodic behavior can be repeated as the number of blades is increased above the two blades in the experiment, and specifically if the number of blades goes above four. Researchers on vortex interactions, related to shear layer transition, have reported mathematical treatments indicating that a system with four or more vortices in proximity will develop chaotic behavior. In the rotor case, the vortices are not completely independent, so it may well be that the progression to chaos if any, does not occur quickly enough to influence the deterministic pairing interactions and the transition to the “far wake” condition. In cases such as the onset of the vortex ring state or the formation of the ground vortex and the development of “brownout” in dusty environments, several turns of the tip vortices may indeed remain close enough for chaotic behavior to be significant. Such phenomena remain to be investigated. However, the visible proof of deterministic behavior for a two-bladed rotor encouraged Jain and Conlisk (Ref. 8) to undertake calculations of wake vortex behavior to long wake age and to study mutual interactions of the vortices. They were able to capture the mutual interaction of the vortices observed in the 1996 Ames experiments and the resulting vortex trajectory. This capability allowed them to obtain better agreement with other published experimental wake trajectories than was possible from prior lifting line/free wake calculations. This also allowed computation of vortex rollup and reingestion phenomena in ground effect. Thus a simple model of deterministic vortex interactions, based on observed physics, has resulted in both efficient and accurate prediction of rotor wakes to large ages.
Vortex core size remains small for large vortex ages, up to several revolutions. Further proof of the long persistence of tip vortices comes from visualization of the density gradient across TV cores. Figure 5 is a NASA Ames shadowgraphy experiment (Ref. 9) on a Sikorsky S-76 rotor at a tip Mach number of 0.605. Thus the highest Mach number difference across the core can only be 0.6, even if the TV starts out at a full strength value computed using potential flow theory from the peak bound circulation at the tip. It is well known that shadowgraphy and schlieren techniques do not work (i.e., do not provide enough contrast to be captured in video) for differences below Mach 0.25. In other words, even after some 360o of rotor age, the vortex strength remains close to its original strength. Coalescing the above two observations, one may conclude that the tip vortices in hover or low-speed climb should stay well organized in deterministic rather than chaotic behavior, and their strength should dissipate very slowly with increasing vortex age, at least in the case of rotors with up to two blades. Figure 6, which summarizes data from several independent experiments, clearly illustrates this behavior. The factor k is used to multiply the peak bound circulation to match the vortex strength data. This appears to be in contradiction to some existing formulations, where a large turbulent diffusion is necessary to explain
