The rolled up structure at the edge of the inboard vortex sheet. As the tip vortices develop along helical trajectories, the inboard blade wake, which is basically a thin, flat shear layer developed from the turbulent boundary layers over the surfaces of the blade, convects down faster than the TV. Interactions with older TV segments may occur. Kim et al. (Ref. 11) demonstrated that in the case of a highly loaded, untwisted blade, the edge of the inboard vortex sheet of the rotor blade rolls up into a counterrotating vortex (CRV), whose strength approached 50% of that of the TV. Experiments by Ghee and Elliott (Ref. 12) at Langley employing a substantially larger (but still model scale) rotor confirmed this finding. While this has not been directly shown in numerical computations, Egolf (Ref. 13) cites calculations that attribute up to a 20% reduction in TV strength to the interaction with the inboard vortex sheet. He also demonstrated by smoke visualization images from UTRC wind tunnel tests and computations that a “dual vortex” or counterrotating vortex pair can be seen above the rotor blade in some flight conditions. The experimental cases of Kim and Ghee established that such a structure must exist in many model-scale experiments at moderate or higher thrust coefficients. This finding will have a profound influence on the relation between the peak-bound circulation on the blade and the strength of the TV. If this presence of a strong rolled up CRV exists for full-scale main rotors at high-thrust coefficients, it should have substantial effects on the structure of the wake. Hence it should also have a strong effect on induced velocities and the thrust that the rotor develops. However, there is no evidence of similar counterrotating vorticity in the computations reported by Narducci (Ref. 14) for a large fixed blade twisted to approximate the circulation distribution of a typical rotor in hover. These computations were correlated to experiments at the Boeing Vertol Wind Tunnel for a vortex shed from a nonrotating generic tiltrotor blade at M∞ = 0.15 and α = 6.5◦. The circulation distribution shown in this case had a small hump near the tip, but this does not appear to generate a visible counterrotating vortex structure at the edge of the inboard sheet. One may postulate that the counterrotating pair phenomenon is due to aspects of the rotation, such as radial acceleration, that are not present in fixed-wing experiments.
The vortex strength paradox. Early applications of the “force-free wake” analysis resulted in a disconnect between the correct force-free wake geometry calculated from the blade loading for known thrust, and the correct thrust calculated using a prescribed (known) wake geometry. Recent results suggest that this can be attributed to a basic misconception in the application of potential flow vortex element (VE) analysis to rotor wakes. Patient application of nonintrusive measurement techniques in facilities where vortex–wall interactions are minimized has enabled clean measurements of vortex core structure in axial and in edgewise flight to large wake ages. This has spurred a retrospective evaluation of data existing in the literature. The traditional assumption that all the vorticity outboard of the bound circulation peak will roll into the TV appears to be too simplistic. A large part of this vorticity is convected into the edge of the inboard vortex sheet. The extent to which this occurs
