that resolves the alternative RANS CFD methods in the wake while preserving conventional RANS CFD in the rotor near field to take advantage of the best characteristics of both methods is a logical step. These methods are collectively known as hybrid methods and are discussed in the following section.
Hybrid Formulations. As noted in the prior sections, each of the computational methods applied to the wake problem has advantages and disadvantages. It was recognized early on in the 1970s and 1980s that a combination of methods that merge the best characteristics of each method, while mitigating the disadvantages, showed promise in tackling the wake-modeling issue. This class of simulations is known collectively as “hybrid” methods. Early efforts attempted to address the cost of the CFD simulation, along with the problematic wake dissipation, by creating a mixed Eulerian–Lagrangian formulation, although this is no longer the case as hybrid methods that couple traditional CFD with alternative CFD methods such as Eulerian VT formulations are being developed. Initial hybrid methods typically utilized the more costly computational methods to capture the blade near-field physics, either with fullpotential methods in the 1970s and 1980s (Refs. 39, 81–83) or more recently, RANS methods (Refs. 84, 85). These hybrid methods will be referred to as CFD-Lagrangian wake methods. Most of these hybrid methods apply free- or prescribed-wake methods to resolve the far wake, reducing the computational grid requirements. However, they achieve this reduction at the expense of the physics, as the limitations in the free wakes still remain. Additional simplifications, such as modeling one blade of the rotor, further reduce the cost of quasi-steady simulations such as hover and steady level flight. TURNS-UMARC has shown very good correlation of airloads for forward flight (Ref. 86), and HELIX versions have been shown to predict figure of merit at or within experimental error bounds (Ref. 38). Issues that are still being investigated for these methods tend to center on boundary condition issues associated with the interface when strong levels of vorticity are exchanged between the methods. More recent hybrid methods that are under development combine the RANS CFD in the blade near field with VT or VC CFD methods in the far field. This hybrid methodology is more consistent than CFD-Lagrangian wake hybrid methods as the Navier–Stokes equations are solved in both the near-field and wake modules. VorTran-M has been coupled with an unstructured solver, RSA3D (Ref. 72), as well as with OVERFLOW and FUN3D, whereas VC has been coupled with TURNS (Ref. 87) and may be coupled with UM-TURNS. Here, the vorticity (or velocity vector) from the wake method can be directly inserted into the CFD grid to alleviate many of the interface issues with which CFD-Lagrangian wake hybrid methods must contend, to conserve the RANS equations. The primary formulations of these vorticity methods still tend to be incompressible, so that density changes must be handled with care to preserve continuity. The current primary drawback with these methods is that their implementation is designed for serial processors, which means that their full effectiveness has not yet been evaluated.
Computational wake modeling issues still to be resolved
While many advancements have been made with computational codes, there are still major wake modeling issues that need to be resolved. Some of the more important questions or observations necessary for wake modeling and the current efforts in the area include the following:
The need for additional research to refine vortex core models. Egolf (Ref. 13) points out that despite large advances in computational methods and computer speed, analytical modeling of rotor wakes continues to be essential due to the high cost of conventional RANS CFD-based
methods. Within the VE methods, the core is modeled via analytical algorithms based on empirical observations. Bhagwat and Leishman (Ref. 88) summarize a number of vortex core models and show how the features of the model can impact hover simulations. However, core modeling remains a fertile area for research, as experimental techniques to measure the behavior of the vortex core are improving with technology advancements. These core models were developed from small-scale experiments, so that there are a number of questions relating the model assumptions to the vortex core behavior. Egolf (Ref. 13) specifically cites the assumption of the initial core sizes and decay rates that are necessary when applying VE methods. Both Egolf and Quackenbush (Ref. 33) also list entrainment or agglomeration behavior in the wake for interactional aerodynamics and brownout as an area for additional research. Expounding on the interactional questions, further understanding of “distinct” vortices (e.g., TV, vortices from abrupt sweep changes), and their interaction with the inboard sheet or with other vortical features of complex blade tip shapes is necessary. For instance, such understanding is essential to correctly model the experimentally observed dual vortices well inboard of the rotor tip, and the rollup of the root vortices. Near-wake core modeling issues can be alleviated by coupling the VE method with a conventional Euler/RANS CFD code that captures the blade vorticity. Here, the analytical modeling is replaced with firstprinciples methods, which, if applied correctly, should provide a more physical representation of the near-field wake. However, these methods have their own limitations and are not a panacea for all near-wake issues.
The role of turbulence modeling in defining the near-field (and mid-/ far-field) wake. Transition and turbulence modeling are the issues that tend to be most often cited when explaining differences between CFD and experimental results. When resolving the flow field about the rotor blade, the shed wake is directly related to the solver’s ability to predict the blade loading and rotor performance. It is this aspect of RANS CFD that has received the bulk of the attention during the CFD development and is summarized very aptly in Strawn et al. (Ref 3). The ability of RANS methods to predict aeroelastic effects will also impact the blade loading and the subsequent strength and location of the prominent wake vortices. Recent CFD/CSD coupling efforts have significantly improved the ability of CFD to predict blade loading (e.g., Refs. 86, 89, 90) and have recently been applied to BVI (Ref. 91) For rotor applications that include transonic flows and stalled flows, it has been shown that algebraic models do not correctly simulate the behavior of rotor airfoils under these conditions (Ref. 92). One- and two-equation models appear to capture the overall behavior of the rotor well, including the TV, for many applications (Refs. 14, 91). However, they miss dynamic stall rotor characteristics (Refs. 89, 93). Advanced turbulence models are gaining attention after they showed promise in the DARPA Quiet Helicopter program (Ref. 89). Nichols et al. (Ref. 94), Szydlowski and Costes (Ref. 93), and Lynch and Smith (Ref. 95) have recently demonstrated calculations of dynamic stall and improvements in capturing complex wake physics using various advances in turbulence simulation techniques. Transition remains a key issue in some simulations, in particularly hover, as noted by Narducci (Ref. 14). Many legacy codes assume fully turbulent flows or have some user-defined transition location, but only a few include transition modeling as part of the turbulent closure methodology. Transition modeling appears to have mixed influence on the prediction of loading for dynamic applications (Refs. 96, 97), improving static or low-frequency integrated loads, but having less effect as the reduced frequency increases. Transition and turbulence modeling issues become even more important when advanced concepts are studied. Active flaps include unsteady
