Conclusions
The state of the art and recommendations for future research paths pertaining to the experimental and computational modeling of rotor wakes have been summarized, building on reviews done from the 1980s through the early 2000s. The reviews have been augmented with discussions by experts from the United States, Europe, and Asia in a March 2009 workshop on the topic of rotor wake modeling. Eight questions were posed at this workshop and subsequently explored in this work. A summary of these questions and conclusions that can now be drawn are as follows: 1) What is the structure of the TV at its origin, and what factors influence it? This question appears to remain unresolved at the present time, and a database that identifies the characteristics of the TV (strength and location of the vortex, and how it varies with blade tip, advance ratio, and other relevant parameters) would be helpful to the community. All of the factors that influence the development of the TV at its origin may not be identified or quantified. Experimental evidence proves that rotorcraft tip vortices persist with small core size for several revolutions of wake age. When facility wall interactions are reduced sufficiently, the wake of a two-bladed rotor does indeed evolve in a deterministic manner. Long timescales and sharp transients appear in ground effect or when other obstacles are present in the wake path. The transition to the far wake occurs through deterministic processes of interaction between vortices, leading to vortex mergers. 2) How much of the blade-bound vorticity actually ends up in the TV? TV strength is substantially lower than the peak-bound circulation. Several published data sets from a wide range of independent experi
ments indicate this range to be 45–50%, whereas other researchers cited additional experiences where ranges of 80–100% were observed. The difference is attributable to ingestion of the tip shear layer into the counterrotating inboard vortex sheet before the TV is formed. Hence, tip shape details are very important. The remainder of the bound vorticity moves inboard, depending on the blade configuration. Thus, depending on the blade configuration, the inboard-bound vorticity may have to be modeled to capture the TV characteristics and hence the correct blade loading. 3) How fast does the TV diffuse/dissipate/decay? What is the role of turbulence in these processes? The inviscid or time-averaged characteristics of the TV are more important for most engineering applications than the details of the turbulence within the vortex. Small-scale turbulence is not of primary issue, and, at present, requires too many assumptions to model computationally. The turbulent structure of the vortex may be important to acoustics and blade interactions, as outside the core there are turbulence effects with some diffusion, whereas the core itself may have relaminarized. 4) What are the physical phenomena responsible for observed “vortex jitter”? As rotor wake vortices are, by definition, not steady, unsteady effects observed in experiments should be expected. Certainly in hover there is pairing of the vortices, so that unsteadiness exists and grows with time. Care should be taken in defining vortex unsteadiness, as facilityinduced unsteadiness has caused some incorrect conclusions to be drawn. For example, in some wind tunnel tests of fixed wing configurations, unsteadiness of the TV has been observed, but it was found later to be due to the interaction of the wake with the unsteadiness of the flow in the wind tunnel.
