5) What is the state of computational capability for the TV as a function of age in hover and forward flight? Wake structures in the blade near field appear to be well captured using classic RANS CFD methods for most flight conditions, except for regions with dynamic stall. In addition, dual vortices that have been observed for several decades in negatively loaded advancing blades, have yet to duplicated (captured) by computational means. Further development of advanced turbulence simulation techniques that are appropriate to RANS production size grids and time requirements are warranted. The influence of transition continues to be an unknown quantity for the prediction of the rotor airloads and wake. It appears from studies thus far that transition is important for steady and low-frequency flight conditions that involve rotor blade stall, but is less important for high-frequency phenomena. 6) How well are long-age phenomena such as ground vortex rollup, and tail rotor/main rotor interaction captured? Using combinations of higher order schemes, grid adaptation, and/or a mix of grid/solver topologies, classic RANS CFD can capture four to five revolutions of the rotor wake without excessive grid requirements. Wake core size and strength can be captured with RANS CFD methods if sufficient grid points for the spatial and temporal scheme are included within the core region. Wakes for longer time periods must be modeled with hybrid methods involving VEs, VT, or VC in the wake. The role of turbulence modeling in long-wake-age vortex cores in combination with the dissipation of the numerical schemes has not yet been clearly defined.
Recommendation
The investment into a new isolated rotor hover test, using state-ofthe-art measurement techniques, to provide an experimental database for correlation with computational methods, including VE-based (comprehensive) codes is warranted. The scatter on prior tests is large, and the data necessary to enable full correlation with numerical methods, linking the wake, blade loading, and overall performance of the rotor are not readily available. This hover test should include a systematic study of the influence of blade planforms and tips, keeping other parameters constant so that conclusions can be concretely drawn from both the experiment and computational correlations.
